{
  "dom_id": "step-q112137",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 9 & -4 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-5x+4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-5x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x-4)=(x-4)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-4)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png)",
